We need to determine the number of distinguishable ways to write the number \(9\) as a sum of \(1\text{'s},\) \(2\text{'s},\) and \(4\text{'s},\) where the order of the addends matters. For instance, the sums \(4 + 4 + 1\) and \(1 + 4 + 4\) are considered different.

### Approach
To solve this problem, we can use a combinatorial approach known as dynamic programming. The idea is to build up the number of ways to form each sum from \(0\) to \(9\) using the available addends \(1\), \(2\), and \(4\).

We define an array `ways` where `ways[n]` represents the number of ways to write the number \(n\) as a sum of the given addends. The base case is that there is exactly one way to form the sum \(0\) (by using no addends), so we initialize `ways[0] = 1`.

